# AI Usage Governance & Cost Optimization Policy

## For Cursor, ChatGPT, Claude, Codex & AI Engineering Workflows

Version: 1.0
Owner: Engineering / Founder
Objective: Reduce unnecessary AI spend while maximizing engineering productivity, output quality, and delivery speed.

---

# 1. Governance Philosophy

AI models are productivity accelerators, not unlimited compute resources.

The objective of this governance policy is to:

* Reduce unnecessary token consumption
* Prevent repeated expensive generations
* Use the correct model for the correct task
* Improve prompt quality and engineering discipline
* Introduce operational cost awareness
* Scale AI-assisted engineering sustainably

Core Principle:

> Use expensive reasoning only when high-value reasoning is required.

---

# 2. Primary Cost Drivers

The largest causes of AI spend are:

* Massive prompt context windows
* Re-generating entire documents repeatedly
* Using premium models for simple tasks
* Multi-agent orchestration loops
* Repeated retries without refinement
* Long architectural discussions
* Uploading large files unnecessarily
* Lack of caching/reuse
* Poor prompt specificity
* Infinite refinement loops

---

# 3. Approved AI Tool Usage Strategy

## 3.1 Model Allocation Policy

### LOW-COST MODELS (Default)

Use for:

* Formatting
* CRUD code generation
* Refactoring
* Documentation cleanup
* Summaries
* UI boilerplate
* Sprint task generation
* Markdown formatting
* Regex/simple scripts

Examples:

* GPT-4o-mini
* Claude Haiku
* Gemini Flash

Rule:

> Default to low-cost models unless advanced reasoning is required.

---

### MID-TIER MODELS

Use for:

* Backend implementation
* API design
* Medium complexity debugging
* Workflow orchestration
* Refined technical writing
* Prompt engineering

Examples:

* GPT-4.1
* Claude Sonnet

Rule:

> Use only after low-cost model limitations are identified.

---

### PREMIUM MODELS

Use ONLY for:

* Architecture decisions
* Complex reasoning
* Multi-system design
* AI orchestration strategy
* Critical debugging
* Security analysis
* Final design reviews
* High-value product decisions

Examples:

* GPT-5.5
* Claude Opus

Rule:

> Premium reasoning must not be used for repetitive engineering tasks.

---

# 4. Prompt Governance Rules

## 4.1 Never Regenerate Entire Documents

BAD:

* Rewrite whole PRD
* Regenerate complete architecture
* Recreate full sprint plan

GOOD:

* Update section 3 only
* Refactor API section only
* Modify authentication workflow only

Expected Savings:
50–80%

---

## 4.2 Context Minimization

Only provide:

* Relevant files
* Relevant logs
* Relevant code blocks

Avoid:

* Entire repositories
* Full architecture dumps
* Unrelated backlog data

Rule:

> Every unnecessary token is paid for repeatedly.

---

## 4.3 Prompt Precision

BAD:
“Fix everything.”

GOOD:
“Fix hydration mismatch in login page caused by server/client rendering inconsistency.”

Expected Savings:

* Faster convergence
* Fewer retries
* Lower token usage

---

## 4.4 Limit Iterative Loops

Maximum:

* 3 iterations per problem before reframing prompt

If issue persists:

* Simplify
* Isolate root cause
* Reduce context
* Change model

---

